questions.scenario:
You are debugging a production issue where a high-throughput microservice is experiencing severe memory leaks and intermittent `null` lookup failures. A developer implemented a custom session store using `ConcurrentHashMap` with a custom key class:

java
public class UserSessionKey {
    private String userId;
    private String deviceId;

    public UserSessionKey(String userId, String deviceId) {
        this.userId = userId;
        this.deviceId = deviceId;
    }

    public void setDeviceId(String deviceId) {
        this.deviceId = deviceId;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof UserSessionKey)) return false;
        UserSessionKey that = (UserSessionKey) o;
        return Objects.equals(userId, that.userId) && Objects.equals(deviceId, that.deviceId);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(userId, deviceId);
    }
}


When a user updates their device, the background worker invokes `key.setDeviceId(newDeviceId)` on an existing `UserSessionKey` instance while it is actively stored as a key inside the `ConcurrentHashMap`.
